;; Docs for samizdat/cli.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "cli.bjodoc").
;; The texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}, and a literal
;; at sign is @"@".

(:doc #:module
  (summary "The command line of bjo sz, on the functions of (samizdat author).")
  (see sz-main)
  (example """
    ;; .bjo/sz/main.bjo, which bjo sz writes for a project with an sz.bjo.
    (import (std prelude))
    (import (samizdat cli))
    (import (only "../../sz.bjo" configure))

    (defun (main args)
      (sz-main args configure))
    """)
  (reference """
    @code{bjo sz} reads documents and writes them. Each command has its
    own help: @code{bjo sz md --help}.

    @read-table{
    | @code{bjo sz md FILE... [-o OUT] [--check]}   | Markdown (GFM): each @code{FILE.sz} gives @code{FILE.md} beside it |
    | @code{bjo sz html FILE... [-o OUT] [--check]} | HTML pages. @code{--fragment} writes the body only. @code{--css URL} adds a stylesheet |
    | @code{bjo sz build [DIR] [--to md] [-o DIR]}  | every @code{.sz} file below @code{DIR}, as HTML or Markdown |
    | @code{bjo sz check FILE...}                   | read the documents and run their commands. Write nothing |
    | @code{bjo sz outline FILE...}                 | the headings, with the id that @code{@"@"ref} and a @code{#} link take |
    | @code{bjo sz links FILE... [--check]}         | the links and images. @code{--check} shows only the broken ones |
    | @code{bjo sz deps FILE...}                    | the files the documents are made from, for a build tool |
    | @code{bjo sz tree FILE}                       | the document after its commands ran, as s-expressions |
    | @code{bjo sz commands}                        | the commands that a document can use here |
    }

    @itemlist{
    @item{@code{-o -} writes one document to standard output. With more than one @code{FILE}, @code{-o} is a directory.}
    @item{@code{md}, @code{html} and @code{build} also take @code{--heading-offset N} and @code{--no-typography}. @code{md} and @code{html} take @code{--title}. @code{html} takes @code{--lang} and @code{--id-prefix}. @code{build} takes @code{--css} and @code{--lang} for HTML.}
    @item{@code{--check} writes nothing. It exits with 1 when an output is not what its source gives.}
    @item{Messages about the run go to standard error. So @code{-o -} writes only the output to standard output.}
    }

    @strong{sz.bjo.} A project adds its own commands and hooks with an
    @code{sz.bjo} in its root. It exports @code{configure}, a function
    from @code{Setup} to @code{Setup}. @code{bjo sz} gives it
    @code{default-setup}, and then applies the options of the command
    line. So an option overrides @code{sz.bjo}.

    @strong{How it runs.} @code{bjo sz} builds a program whose
    @code{main} calls @code{sz-main}, and runs it in the current directory.
    Without an @code{sz.bjo}, @code{configure} returns the setup it gets.
    """))

(:doc sz-main
  (summary "Parses the words of bjo sz and runs the command.")
  (arg args "The words after @code{sz}.")
  (arg configure "Makes the setup from @code{default-setup}. The options of the command line are applied after it.")
  (returns """
    The exit code: 0 for success and for @code{--help}, 1 when a document
    has an error, an output is not up to date with @code{--check}, or a
    link is broken with @code{links --check}, and 2 for a wrong command
    line.
    """)
  (see Setup default-setup)
  (example """
    (sz-main ["md" "--check" "README.sz"] (fun (s) s))
    """)
  (reference """
    The help goes to standard output. Each other message, and the error
    of a wrong command line, goes to standard error.
    """))
